<DocsLayout title="What is still open" description="A register of every open item in the scmle plan, sorted into four classes: buildable here, needs outside people or data, needs a daemon or a real deployment, and needs an owner decision, with the counts." current="open-items">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Reference</p>
  <h1>What is still open,<br /><em>and why.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">The plan lists every remaining open item once, with a class and the evidence for it, grouped under four stages: trustworthy evidence, autonomous operation, methods and instrument, and external validation and release. This page summarises that register. It is a status record, not a promise of dates.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Read it as a limit.</strong> Most open items are not missing code. They need people, data, tools or decisions that the author did not have. None of them has been closed by saying so.</p></div>

  <h2>The four classes</h2>
  <DocTable label="Classes of open item and their row counts" head={["Class", "Meaning", "Rows"]} rows={classes} />

  <h2>Counts</h2>
  <p>The register lists 139 open rows: 53 class A, 52 class B, 11 class C and 23 class D. By stage: 3 in trustworthy evidence, 5 in autonomous operation, 43 in methods and instrument, and 88 in external validation and release (which also holds the container path, trail and breadth items). A row that needs both local work and outside input is split into two rows, so each row has exactly one class. A built or closed item is deleted from the register; the record is the changelog.</p>

  <h2>What class A still needs</h2>
  <p>The class A items that remain are open because of size, not because they need anything outside the machine. They are statistical methods that each need their own pre-registered design, certificate and re-issue (for example the remaining label-noise, sentinel and per-period drift detectors, difference-in-differences, regression discontinuity and synthetic control, policy trees, intervals for the other task types, and a re-registered closed-loop alarm design). They also include the controller that would run the loop unattended, the repository-wide mutation run, which takes many hours, and the 3.11 and 3.14 identity of the ingestion and causal reports.</p>

  <h2>What needs outside people or data (class B)</h2>
  <p>The largest block is validation that the author cannot supply: a backtest on real datasets with a time axis, a red team by an analyst who did not write the instrument, an independent replication, the external AutoML campaign, reference outputs from R packages (R is not installed on the author&rsquo;s machine), a domain-owner pilot, and CI observations that exist only after a push. This is why the instrument is described as built and tested but not independently validated, and why no AutoML head-to-head result exists (see <a href={`${base}/docs/benchmarks/#what-this-shows`}>Benchmarks</a>).</p>

  <h2>What needs a daemon or a real deployment (class C)</h2>
  <p>Any step that needs a container daemon, an image scanner, a real deployment or a real database role is open. This is why the container path is described as untested end to end (see <a href={`${base}/docs/local/`}>Local environment</a>).</p>

  <h2>What needs an owner decision (class D)</h2>
  <p>The choice of version for the next release, its changelog heading, tag and supported-versions text, and the pre-registered protocol for the qualification comparison, with its claim, corpus rule, thresholds and stop rules, wait on the owner.</p>

  <h2>State of the evidence</h2>
  <p>The continuous-integration run on the head that this register was written against (<code>f453091</code>) is red: the Windows jobs fail a replay-identity test because text artifacts are written with Windows line endings, and the scheduled full-suite run fails the oracle-table test on Linux. The last head with a fully green run is <code>9d4bc04</code>. Making the evidence trustworthy is the first stage.</p>

  <p class="meta">Counts as recorded by the plan register of 2026-11-08</p>
</DocsLayout>
